In <3ie3j4$f59@news.unimelb.EDU.AU> Richard Begg <richard@pitlochry.its.unimelb.EDU.AU> writes:
>We have a need for an otherwise non-privilaged user to bind
>specific privilaged ports (e.g. 80).  Is there any way this
>can be done without full root access.  

No.

The safest thing to do is provide a small setuid wrapper program that
opens the port, binds to it, and then passes the open descriptor down
to the child program as a command-line parameter.  For an example,
see "inndstart" in the INN distribution if you have it lying around.
	/r$


