Doug Christensen <doug@alviso.consilium.com> wrote:
> Can anyone point me in the right direct to help in getting information
> regarding the differences and similiarities between DCE and CORBA.

This newsgroup is one source, "comp.object" is another.  I suspect
that some of your questions would be answered by the comp.object FAQ.

Be aware that some vendors are a bit touchy about these topics, given
that the OMG (including end users) decided not to require use of DCE
in all CORBA implementations.


> My questions are very basic and fundamental in nature:
>
> What is DCE?

It's a group of licensed "Distributed Computing Environment" (DCE)
technologies adopted by OSF (the "Open Software Foundation", a computer
industry consortium) including a file system, an RPC, some security
services, and more.  There's even an overview published about those
technologies.  I'm sure someone can provide a more complete overview.

One thing to keep in mind is that many people aren't interested in all of
OSF's DCE, but only selected parts of it.  When you talk about "DCE" you
should probably be clear about whether you mean the whole body of code
(expensive, among other things) or specific components.  If you mean one
thing by "DCE" and someone else means a different thing, you won't
communicate as effectively as you'd like.


(I have called these "licensed technologies" NOT to deny that some of the
specifications are available and could be reimplemented without any license
fee, but to emphasize that as a rule that's not done.  Even Microsoft had
a tough time doing it, and isn't [yet?] completely compatible -- they are the
main example of an unlicensed implementation.  And while there is now a
version of DCE-RPC source that is "freely licenced", successful use of that
code is evidently rare; it's not [yet?] portable by current standards.)


> What is CORBA?

It's a group of "Common Object Request Broker Architecture" (CORBA)
interface specifications adopted by OMG (the "Object Management Group", a
different computer industry consortium), part of a family of specifications
fleshing out the "Object Management Architecture" (OMA).

CORBA defines an object model based on a particular interface definition
language (OMG-IDL), how clients talk to an object, and core interfaces used
to and activate implement objects.  There is also a suite of dynamic typing
facilities, usable both to invoke and to implement objects without any
compile-time support.  It defines language mappings from OMG-IDL to C, C++,
and Smalltalk, and how to talk to an ORB using the Internet's TCP protocol.

The "Common Object Services" (COS) facilities include naming, transactions,
events, properties, relationships, and more; those are part of the OMA but
not CORBA.  They are also called the "CORBA Object Services" due to their
close coupling to ORBs; COS services are also part of the OMA, and are
sometimes (loosely) talked of as part of CORBA.


> What are the differences/similarities?

Key similarities are at the level of "full DCE" and the OMA of which CORBA
is a part.  Both support construction of heterogeneous distributed
systems with a full set of development facilities such as naming and
security.  (The OMG is right now working on its security infrastructure;
most observers expect it to allow use of some DCE security components,
but not to require them, and to provide key additional functionality.)

Key differences are that DCE didn't start out by being object oriented, and
didn't include nondistributed infrastructure in its original plans.  So
for example you can be CORBA-compliant but not support networking.  The
dynamic typing facilities in CORBA have no DCE analogue, and the services
defined in each environment don't fully overlap.  In my opinion, systems
based on CORBA will scale better since CORBA accepts strong boundaries
between subsystems (e.g. administrative domains) at its most basic level.

Also, the processes used to develop/evolve the two are different, and
are leading to different kinds of products.  While most DCE products are
ports of the OSF/DCE code, ORB products as a rule have no common code
base.  This allows more efficient customer input into feature sets.


> Is it possible to develop a system which conforms to both standards?

Absolutely, but it's not required.

One way to do it is to build a CORBA 2.0 compliant product atop some set of
DCE facilities; some vendors are planning this sort of ORB.  Another way to
do it is to bundle a set of DCE facilities with a CORBA 2.0 compliant ORB,
so the ORB doesn't use them but (nonportable) applications could use both
sets of facilities.

Yet another way is to find some way to integrate selected features.  You
could perhaps have an ORB that's tuned to do ORB things very efficiently,
yet uses a site's pre-existing authentication system (e.g. perhaps the
DCE-ized Kerberos) instead of defining its own such system.

--
