In article <20744@cimshop.UUCP>, Doug Christensen (doug@alviso.consilium.com) wrote:
> Can anyone point me in the right direct to help in getting information regarding
> the differences and similiarities between DCE and CORBA.

For some bizarre reason, I feel like taking a shot at these
questions...

> What is DCE?

Distributed Computing Environment, from OSF, sold for/by HP, IBM,
Digital, SUN, MS Windows, ...

> What is CORBA?

Common Object Request Broker Architecture (I think)

> What are the differences/similarities?

Similarities:  Both start with an "interface definition language".  An
IDL is a syntax for describing the interface among distributed
components of a distributed application.  In both cases, the interface
is essentially a description of a collection of function calls, using
C-like syntax, including function name, IN and OUT paramaters, and
return values.

An IDL compiler is used to translate the IDL description into actual
source code that can be compiled, linked, and executed.  In principle,
one server could be generated on one architecture, another on some other
architecture, and clients on yet another, and they will all
interoperate.  NOTE that I said "in principle".

In addition, there needs to be some standard set of infrastructure
services, providing services such as name/location/binding, security,
and so on.  Of course, it all has to run over some network or other, but
both DCE and CORBA try to be independent of the underlying transport.

Differences:

CORBA's IDL is more OO.  It has a natural inheritance syntax.

DCE interoperates better.  (All DCE implementations interoperate.  Few
CORBA implementations do.)




> Is it possible to develop a system which conforms to both standards?

Yes, but it would probably be two interfaces folded into one program.
That is, the underlying functionality could be shared, but the CORBA and
DCE parts would be quite separate.


> Thank you very much for any help in this area

You're welcome, as are any followup comments.  FLames to /dev/null

